  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a logarithm?",
      "a": "A logarithm tells you what power you need. Log base 10 of 1000 is 3, because 10 to the power 3 is 1000. It counts how many times you multiply by 10.",
      "asks": [
        "Explain logs simply",
        "What does log mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the log of 1 zero?",
      "a": "You reach 1 by multiplying by 10 zero times. Any number to the power 0 is 1, so log of 1 is 0 in every base.",
      "asks": [
        "What is log 1?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is log 1000 equal to 3?",
      "a": "Because 10 times 10 times 10 is 1000. That is three tens multiplied, so 10 to the power 3.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you work out log 1000?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is log of a number between 10 and 100?",
      "a": "It is between 1 and 2. For example, log 50 is about 1.7, because 10 to the power 1.7 is about 50.",
      "asks": [
        "Can a log be a decimal?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a logarithm with a different base?",
      "a": "The base is the number you multiply by. Log base 2 of 8 is 3, because 2 times 2 times 2 is 8.",
      "asks": [
        "What is log base 2?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "log₁₀ 1000 = 3 because 10³ = 1000.",
    "The logarithm of 1 is 0 in every base, because any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1."
